#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134701} :activity/id #uuid "c184b7bf-d80e-522a-9bde-709e111cc316" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049845 :topic/id #uuid "183bab17-91bb-560a-b6e6-758a6cc0dfcd" :topic/math-academy-id 2168} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135061 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the least-squares problem for the system\n\n$$A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nWe can use QR factorization to construct the least-squares solution of this system. Recall that finding the QR factorization of $A$ means expressing $A$ in the form\n\n$$A = QR,$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $Q$ has orthonormal columns, and\n- $R$ is an upper triangular matrix with positive entries on its main diagonal.\n\nTo see how QR factorization can be used to solve a least-squares problem, we first recall that the least-squares solution satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$A^T A \\mathbf{x} = A^T \\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nSubstituting $A = QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, we have $Q^T Q = I.$ Therefore, the above equation becomes\n\n$$R^TR\\mathbf{x} = R^TQ^T\\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nSince $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying our equation by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}$$ which simplifies as\n\n$$R \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}. \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)$$\n\nThe least-squares solution $\\hat{\\mathbf x}$ can now be found by solving this equation using back-substitution or row operations. This is usually the fastest way.\n\nIf we want an explicit formula for the least-squares solution, then we note that since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying $(\\ast)$ by $R^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\\mathbf{x} = R^{-1} Q^T \\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nThe least-squares solution is unique if $A$ has linearly independent columns." :tutorial/id #uuid "7a9501fc-fdbc-5905-ad31-e3c6c2767a33" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3639 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "733946c8-bb91-5523-9be6-6e334aa51df6" :step/math-academy-id 20276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133612 :question/id #uuid "5fb2571c-2ab9-5fe5-9454-f9d7b0e89854" :question/math-academy-id "e-5592"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388953}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"} :step/id #uuid "eeb32ce1-92f7-5c9a-8d57-a6fb5f276cdf" :step/math-academy-id 20278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133719 :question/id #uuid "d805b370-4377-5101-8ac7-3d30e11156ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9917"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7d226d3-f7d3-574f-94f9-a532ec99b0ac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050130}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401097}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Expression for a Least-Squares Solution in Terms of the Matrices Q and R"} :step/id #uuid "eab53e14-a261-5ebb-9ee3-6d39662a78cf" :step/math-academy-id 37297 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135143 :tutorial/content "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and $$Q = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}], \\qquad R = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}], \\qquad \\mathbf{b} = [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$R \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$${\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is $[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5fcac262-421e-57b7-b038-713427320ff4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6150 :tutorial/title "Using QR Factorization to Solve a Least-Squares Problem"} :step/id #uuid "f3959285-3101-555f-96a2-bd9e95bfe12e" :step/math-academy-id 37330 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134702}}] :activity/title "Finding a Least-Squares Solution Using QR Factorization" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134028}]}
